        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Ge'ez</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 300 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Family</span><span class="info-stat-val">Semitic: a South Semitic language; on another view, part of an independent Ethio-Semitic branch, in its northern half with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a> and Tigre</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Writing</span><span class="info-stat-val">Ethiopic, the Ge'ez abugida (called fidäl in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a>), developed for the language and read left to right; early inscriptions also in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> script, from the 4th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Earliest records</span><span class="info-stat-val">Inscriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a> from as early as the 4th century, about a dozen long and over 200 short; the oldest known example of the script on the Hawulti <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> at Matara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, tentatively c. 400; the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, the second Garima Gospel, of the 5th or 6th century</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Status today</span><span class="info-stat-val">No longer spoken in daily life (no longer a living language by the 14th century); the main liturgical language of the Ethiopian and Eritrean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Tewahedo Churches, the Ethiopian and Eritrean Catholic Churches and the Beta Israel, used in prayer and public celebrations</span></div>
