    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ge'ez was the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a>, carved on stone from the 4th century in a script made for it, which today also writes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a> and most other languages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</li><li>Its Bible of 81 books, translated mostly from Greek in the first century of the Aksumite church, keeps the only complete text of the Book of Enoch.</li><li>No longer a living language by the 14th century, it remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>’s official written language until the 19th, much like Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and is still the liturgical language of the Ethiopian and Eritrean churches and the Beta Israel.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Apostolic Tradition, an early Christian text attributed to Hippolytus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, is lost in its original Greek; its Ge'ez translation, made in the late 5th century, is the best surviving witness to it.</p></aside>
    </section>

